#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221137 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff3452-19b6-566a-8b39-ad880dee8858" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Effects of the Horizontal Scaling on Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "b8658f47-7f42-530a-a62e-1cc3144059aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21835" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $[MATH: x\\in [-L,L)]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb R$.\nGiven that $L=2$ and $f(x) = \\cosh{(x+1)}$, find the $4$th cosine and the $4$th sine coefficients." :question/worked-solution "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by\n\n$$\nk \\neq 0\n$$\n\nrepresents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{k}\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\ns=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x\n$$\n\nThen, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L$, the function\n\n$$\ng(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of\n\n$$\n2L \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{L} = 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns)\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{0} &= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}g(s)ds \\\\\na_{n} &= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}g(s)\\cos (ns)ds, n \\ge 1 \\\\\nb_{n} &= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}g(s)\\sin (ns)ds, n \\ge 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ns=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x\n$$\n\nand using the fact that\n\n$$\ng(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)\n$$\n\nwe get the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)+ b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4$th cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}g(s)\\cos (4s)ds \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}f(\\frac{L}{π}s)\\cos (4s)ds \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{π} \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}x)\\cos (4 \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}x)d(\\frac{π}{L}x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π} \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\cos (\\frac{4π}{L}x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\cos (\\frac{4π}{L}x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, $4$th sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{4} &= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}g(s)\\sin (4s)ds \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{- π}^{π}f(\\frac{L}{π}s)\\sin (4s)ds \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{π} \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}x)\\sin (4 \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}x)d(\\frac{π}{L}x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π} \\cdot \\frac{π}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\sin (\\frac{4π}{L}x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\sin (\\frac{4π}{L}x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=2$ and\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\cosh(x+1)\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\cosh (x + 1)\\cos (\\frac{4π}{2}x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\cosh (x + 1)\\cos (2πx)dx, \\\\\nb_{4} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\cosh (x + 1)\\sin (\\frac{4π}{2}x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\cosh (x + 1)\\sin (2πx)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221138 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb301102-1a82-5a81-bb11-b9fc56cd1dc7" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term For the Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "ba1cc59e-66ed-58fc-a494-b949c7ed0a88" :question/math-academy-id "e-21836" :question/problem "$f(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)$\nLet $F(x)=3x^2-4x+1$ for $[MATH: x\\in[-2,2).]$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_0$." :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2-4x+1\n$$\n\nfor $[MATH: x \\in [-2,2).]$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{0} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}(3x^{2} - 4x + 1)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[x^{3} - 2x^{2} + x]_{-2}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}((2^{3} - 2 \\cdot 2^{2} + 2) - ((-2)^{3} - 2 \\cdot (-2)^{2} + (-2))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2 - (-18)) \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221139 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3315887-ff5c-5fd7-93bd-a58800e378b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Coefficients For the Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "823fc00b-48af-5e79-9dcc-5e519e81cd4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21837" :question/problem "$f(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}x\\right)$\nLet $F(x)=-5x+4$ for $[MATH: x\\in[-2,2).]$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_3$." :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and\n\n$$\nf(x)=-5x+4\n$$\n\nfor $[MATH: x \\in [-2,2).]$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_3 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2} (-5x+4)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u =-5x + 4 & ⇒u^{′} =-5 \\\\ v^{′} = \\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}x) & ⇒v = \\frac{2}{3π}\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}x)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{3} &= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}(uv\\right|_{-2}^{2} - \\int_{-2}^{2}vu^{′}dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3π}[(-5x + 4)\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}x)]_{-2}^{2} - \\int_{-2}^{2}\\frac{2}{3π}\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}x) \\cdot (-5)dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3π}[(-5(2) + 4)\\sin (3π) - (-5(-2) + 4)\\sin (-3π)] + \\frac{10}{3π}\\int_{-2}^{2}\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}x)dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3π} \\cdot 0 + \\frac{10}{3π} \\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3π})[\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}x)]_{-2}^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{20}{9π^{2}}[\\cos (3π) - \\cos (-3π)]) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{10}{9π^{2}}(-1 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3315887-ff5c-5fd7-93bd-a58800e378b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Coefficients For the Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9582c5ab-396b-59bf-a657-eebc3fbdae08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9582c5ab-396b-59bf-a657-eebc3fbdae08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232897} :answer-field/id #uuid "81ab4dbb-55d1-5118-81c9-04be76994851" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220737}] :question/id #uuid "0ed7ffbf-06f9-50a7-bc59-05cb4e317a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-340873" :question/problem "$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπ}{2}x) + b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπ}{2}x)$\nThe Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function\n$[MATH: F(x) = {2, \\mid x ∈ [0, 2); - 1, \\mid x ∈ [- 2, 0)]$\nis shown above. Find the exact value of $a_{3}$.$a_{3} =$ {{field-1}}"}]}
